{"name":"Tlāloc Blue","hex":"#316f82","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tláloc","blocks":[{"text":"Tláloc is the god of rain in Aztec religion. He was also a deity of earthly fertility and water, and worshipped as a giver of life and sustenance; many rituals and sacrifices predicated upon these aspects were held in his name. He was feared—albeit not as a malicious figure—for his power over hail, thunder, lightning, and rain. He is also associated with caves, springs, and mountains, most specifically the sacred mountain where he was believed to reside. Cerro Tláloc is very important in understanding how rituals surrounding this deity played out. Tláloc appears to be one of the oldest and most universal figures of worship in ancient Mexico."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Tlaloc_glyph.svg/330px-Tlaloc_glyph.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tl%C3%A1loc","wikidata":"Q232870"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Tl%C3%A1loc_4.jpg/500px-Tl%C3%A1loc_4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Tl%C3%A1loc_4.jpg/500px-Tl%C3%A1loc_4.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tláloc in the Codex Borgia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATl%C3%A1loc_4.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Gulf_Coast_Polychrome_Ceramic_Brazier_with_Head_of_Rain_God_Tlaloc.jpg/500px-Gulf_Coast_Polychrome_Ceramic_Brazier_with_Head_of_Rain_God_Tlaloc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Gulf_Coast_Polychrome_Ceramic_Brazier_with_Head_of_Rain_God_Tlaloc.jpg/1280px-Gulf_Coast_Polychrome_Ceramic_Brazier_with_Head_of_Rain_God_Tlaloc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Gulf_Coast_Polychrome_Ceramic_Brazier_with_Head_of_Rain_God_Tlaloc.jpg/500px-Gulf_Coast_Polychrome_Ceramic_Brazier_with_Head_of_Rain_God_Tlaloc.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A brazier depicting Tláloc from Ozuluama, Classic Veracruz culture.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGulf_Coast_Polychrome_Ceramic_Brazier_with_Head_of_Rain_God_Tlaloc.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Cod